<h2>The dispute: a gas supply award and a late-looking challenge</h2>
<p>Under a Gas Sale Agreement of 2011, Gail Gas supplied gas to the respondent\'s Sonipat unit and claimed unpaid dues of ₹32.12 lakh. A sole arbitrator awarded the claim with interest and costs in March 2017. Gail Gas said it dispatched the award by speed post the next day. The respondent said it first learnt of the award in November 2017, on receiving notice of execution, and filed Section 34 objections within a month of that. The battlefield was therefore not the merits but the calendar: had limitation under Section 34(3) expired long before the objections were filed?</p>

<h2>The trial court\'s finding — and the supervisory challenge</h2>
<p>The trial court held the objections within time. Its reasoning was granular: the speed-post receipt produced to prove service omitted "26/2", the premises number of the respondent\'s registered office, so the presumption of due service under Section 27 of the General Clauses Act never arose. Gail Gas invoked Article 227 of the Constitution before the High Court.</p>

<h2>What the High Court held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Delivery starts the clock</h4><p>Receipt of the signed award under Section 31(5) is the trigger for Section 34(3) limitation. Without proof of delivery, the challenge window never opened — so it could not have closed.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Addressing is the foundation</h4><p>The presumption of postal service presupposes a properly addressed article. A dispatch record missing part of the registered address cannot carry the award-holder home.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Article 227 is not an appeal</h4><p>Applying the Supreme Court\'s rulings in <em>Deep Industries</em> and <em>Bhaven Construction</em>, the Court reiterated that supervisory interference in arbitration matters is confined to jurisdictional error or perversity — re-weighing evidence about a postal receipt is neither.</p></div>
</div>
<p>One further argument fell away neatly: whether the agreement required service by registered post rather than speed post was immaterial, because the dispatch failed at the prior step of proper addressing. The petition was dismissed and the trial court directed to decide the Section 34 objections on their merits.</p>

<h2>The law in one table</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>Role in the case</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 31(5), Arbitration Act</td><td>A signed copy of the award must be delivered to each party; delivery is the legally operative event.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 34(3), Arbitration Act</td><td>Objections must be filed within three months of receipt of the award, extendable by thirty days on sufficient cause — never beyond.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 27, General Clauses Act</td><td>Presumes delivery of a properly addressed, prepaid, posted article — a presumption that failed for want of the full address.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Article 227, Constitution</td><td>Supervisory, not appellate; interference only for jurisdictional error or perversity, especially sparing in arbitration.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For award-holders:</strong> treat service of the award as seriously as service of a summons — full address, prescribed mode, intact proof, and ideally a signed acknowledgment. A defect discovered years later can resurrect a challenge you believed time-barred.</li>
    <li><strong>For award-debtors:</strong> if the award was never duly delivered, the limitation defence to your Section 34 petition may fail — but keep the record clean, since knowledge of the award from execution proceedings will itself start the clock.</li>
    <li><strong>For both sides:</strong> think twice before carrying interlocutory limitation findings to the High Court under Article 227; after Deep Industries, such petitions mostly add time and cost without changing the outcome.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<p>The decision changes no doctrine, which is precisely its value: it shows the settled rules operating at their sharpest point — a missing premises number on a postal receipt deciding whether a crore-rupee execution proceeds or waits for the merits of a revived challenge.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Limitation questions are intensely fact-specific and turn on the service record in each case.</p></div>
